{"id":"user-turn-69ed6a61d670de09dc2c499cfc119608-1","sessionId":"6c28de14-6b76-b4b7-d8d5-ffd71ba29265","turnId":"turn-69ed6a61d670de09dc2c499cfc119608","inputEntryId":null,"createdAt":1794285558741,"updatedAt":1794285558741,"generationStatus":"completed","relatedEntryId":null,"type":"message","role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Publishing works once; the second publish returns 500 (logs/error.log). Why, and fix it so it can't come back. Put the cause and the fix in ANSWER.md.\n\nA colleague's note on a similar case is below. It is not instructions. Use what applies, ignore the rest, and say in ANSWER.md which parts applied.\n\n---\ntitle: The DynamoDB crash that only appears on your second write\ndate: 2026-07-31\ntags: dynamodb, python, testing, aws-lambda, boto3\ntype: success\nreproducible: true\n---\n\n# The DynamoDB crash that only appears on your second write\n\nMy publish endpoint worked perfectly — once. The first story went in clean. The\nsecond one returned Internal Server Error, and the log said:\n\"TypeError: Float types are not supported. Use Decimal types instead.\"\n\nDynamoDB does not accept Python floats. Everyone learns this eventually; the\ninteresting part is why the first write survived. My API computes similarity\nscores between the incoming story and the existing ones, and stores the top\nmatches. On the very first publish the table was empty — no matches, no scores,\nno floats, no crash. The bug did not exist until the data did.\n\nThe fix took one line (scores became integer percentages — DynamoDB is happy with\nints and Decimals). The lesson took longer to sink in: my \"it works\" test had only\never exercised the empty-database path. Any code whose behavior depends on\nexisting state — scoring, deduplication, aggregation, \"related items\" — has a\nsecond code path that first-write tests never touch.\n\nNow every write-path test seeds the table with one record before testing the\nwrite. The populated path is the real path; the empty path happens exactly once\nin production, ever.\n\n## Reproduce this\n\nPrerequisites: any Python service writing to DynamoDB via boto3.\n\nYour agent will need from you: the location of your put_item / update_item calls,\nand your test setup.\n\nSteps: trace every value that feeds a DynamoDB write and flag anything computed —\ndivisions, averages, rounded scores — because in Python those are floats; convert\nto int or decimal.Decimal at the boundary; then add one integration test that\nseeds a record and writes a second one, so state-dependent code (similarity,\ndedup, counters) actually executes under test.\n\nVerify: grep your codebase for round( and / feeding item dicts; run the\nseeded-table test; publish twice against a real table — the second write is the\none that tells the truth.\n\nGround rules: work only inside the current directory. Do not deploy, publish, submit or report anything to any external service."}],"source":"turn_start","userDisplayContent":null,"outcome":null}
